  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nClouds are becoming more scattered this afternoon as the low \npressure departs to the east and high pressure moves in. \nNortheasterly flow over Lake Superior is leading to some light \nradar returns that could be dropping rain and snow at the \nsurface along the South Shore. This activity will slowly \ncontinue east as the low moves away from the area, with any \naccumulations remaining very light. Heading into tonight, \nstratus is expected to fill back in across the Northland due to \nlingering low level moisture and decreasing temperatures.\n\nWarm air advection returns to the region on Friday on the back side\nof the departing high pressure system. Low level stability leftover\nfrom Thursday will linger into Friday. Depending on how well the\ncloud cover can clear and mix out throughout the day, we may \nsee mixing into very dry air aloft. This will bring relative \nhumidity values down throughout the afternoon. Temperatures \nwill also trend warmer than normal to close out the work week.\n\nMoving into the weekend, a dry system will move through the area on\nFriday night. Moisture associated with this shortwave is fairly\nminimal, mainly resulting in increased cloud cover as opposed \nto any rainfall. A cold front will then sweep through the region\nearly Saturday morning, keeping temperatures cooler. Despite \nthe frontal passage, conditions will remain completely dry \nacross the region.\n\nThe slightly cooler air will be short-lived, as a mild and warm\nair mass quickly returns to the area by early next week. The \npattern remains largely dry through the middle of the week with \nno significant precipitation chances on the horizon. Global \nmodels keep the primary storm track situated well to our north \nor focused on the coasts, allowing our local area to enjoy quiet\nweather and above normal temperatures through the extended \nperiod.",
